diff --git a/src/requests/adapters.py.md b/src/requests/adapters.py.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..7bb1548
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/requests/adapters.py.md
@@ -0,0 +1,25 @@
+---
+title: "adapters.py"
+date: 2026-09-23
+hash: "sha256:37b93fdeed55f9171681ad4717b89eeaccaf51af94612873110d13b38b57ab62"
+---
+## Decisions
+- A session's default timeout lives on `HTTPAdapter` (`HTTPAdapter(timeout=...)`, mounted on the session),
+  not on `Session`. The product need was "set a default timeout once for a session"; the product owner
+  kept the standing decision in `sessions.py.md` that a timeout is connection behaviour owned by the
+  transport adapters, not Session state (2026-09-23). This rules out `Session.timeout` or any timeout
+  merging in `Session.request`/`send` as the way to meet the need.
+- On an adapter that has a default, a call's `timeout=None` means "use the default"; a call that wants no
+  limit passes `(None, None)` (or urllib3 `Timeout(None, None)`). Two reasons. `Session.request` forwards
+  `timeout=None` for a call that omitted it too, so the adapter cannot tell "omitted" from "explicit None";
+  telling them apart would need a sentinel in `Session`, which the decision above rules out. And wrapper
+  code often passes `timeout=None` through; if that switched the default off, the call would hang, which
+  is exactly what the default is for. This rules out reading `None` as "no limit" on an adapter that has
+  a default. On an adapter without a default, `None` still means no limit, as before.
+
+## Discussions
+- `add_headers()` and the response hooks still receive the caller's raw `timeout` (often `None`), not the
+  effective one. We left this alone to keep the hook contract. Revisit it only if a subclass or hook
+  genuinely needs to see the timeout that was applied.
+- Only `HTTPAdapter` has the default; `BaseAdapter` does not. A custom adapter decides its own timeout
+  behaviour, as it did before this change.
